WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.131 --> 00:00:06.394
<v Daniel>Imagine diagnosing a patient with a terminal illness and telling them they have exactly five years to live.</v>

2
00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:08.435
<v Maya>Right. A really grim prognosis.</v>

3
00:00:08.735 --> 00:00:23.682
<v Daniel>Exactly. And you fully expect them to just slowly decline. But five years pass. You call them in for a scan. And not only are they perfectly alive, but their entire biology has just completely mutated. Like</v>

4
00:00:23.782 --> 00:00:28.344
<v Daniel>they've sprouted a third arm. They don't sleep anymore. And they've somehow learned to play the piano.</v>

5
00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:30.203
<v Maya>It is quite the visual.</v>

6
00:00:30.544 --> 00:00:30.764
<v Daniel>It is.</v>

7
00:00:31.184 --> 00:00:38.291
<v Maya>But yeah, that bizarre mix of relief and just utter confusion, that's exactly what it feels like to look at the labor market right now.</v>

8
00:00:38.692 --> 00:00:48.301
<v Daniel>Because the terminal diagnosis in this case was that widespread panic-inducing prediction that artificial intelligence was going to basically wipe out half the workforce.</v>

9
00:00:48.619 --> 00:01:02.835
<v Maya>Right. Yeah. We were told all these jobs were simply going to vanish. But when we actually look at the anatomy of the labor market today, well, the patient didn't die. It just completely restructured itself in ways those original doctors, you know, they just never saw coming.</v>

10
00:01:03.253 --> 00:01:18.059
<v Daniel>And so if you are, say, building a strategic plan right now, or if you're trying to figure out what to put on a slide to show how we prepare the next generation of students for the workforce, you really need to know what actually happened when those apocalyptic predictions collided</v>

11
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:18.800
<v Daniel>with reality.</v>

12
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:19.700
<v Maya>You absolutely do.</v>

13
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:32.886
<v Daniel>So today for our Deep Dive, we are opening up a brand new, just released in July 2026, working paper from the Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago. It's by Brody, Dunson, and Barr. And it's this fantastic ex post reality check.</v>

14
00:01:33.173 --> 00:01:41.295
<v Maya>Yeah, they actually looked at the hard labor data from 2019 to 2024 to see if that whole automation doom narrative actually held up.</v>

15
00:01:41.395 --> 00:01:50.977
<v Daniel>Which is so refreshing. But to make sense of what this new Chicago Fed paper proves, we brought in a stack of background sources to act as like our historical baseline.</v>

16
00:01:51.578 --> 00:02:00.840
<v Maya>Because you can't understand the present without the past. So we're looking at that legendary 2017 Frey and Osborne paper, the one that basically started the whole panic.</v>

17
00:02:01.065 --> 00:02:02.185
<v Daniel>Oh, yeah. Everyone cited that one.</v>

18
00:02:02.345 --> 00:02:16.188
<v Maya>Right. Plus, we're bringing in crucial context from a 2024 paper by Alondu on language models, a 2025 study by Tomlinson on Microsoft Copilot, and this massive new 2026 data set from Hernes and Kustel in Norway.</v>

19
00:02:16.508 --> 00:02:22.109
<v Daniel>So kind of a full global picture of what we expected versus the profound restructuring we actually got.</v>

20
00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:23.869
<v Maya>Exactly. That's our mission today.</v>

21
00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:36.699
<v Daniel>Okay. So to understand the reality check, we really have to understand the original panic. Let's go back to 2017. Frey and Osborne published this study, and it just sets the modern narrative.</v>

22
00:02:36.899 --> 00:02:37.979
<v Maya>Oh, it was everywhere.</v>

23
00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:50.445
<v Daniel>Yeah. They estimated the probability of computerization for 702 different occupations. And their headline finding, the one that made everyone lose their minds, was that roughly 47% of U.S. jobs were at high risk.</v>

24
00:02:50.885 --> 00:02:55.847
<v Maya>Right. And they defined high risk as a 70% chance or more of automation.</v>

25
00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:06.949
<v Daniel>But looking back at that now, I have to ask, like, what did a probability of computerization actually mean back in 2017? Because, I mean, were they claiming 47 percent of jobs would just evaporate overnight?</v>

26
00:03:07.369 --> 00:03:14.935
<v Maya>No. And that's where the media really kind of misinterpreted them. They weren't giving us an expiration date. They were looking at engineering capabilities.</v>

27
00:03:15.235 --> 00:03:17.117
<v Daniel>OK, so how did they actually build that model then?</v>

28
00:03:17.705 --> 00:03:28.312
<v Maya>Well, they took 70 occupations and they had machine learning experts hand label them, basically saying, you know, is this automatable or not? And then they ran a classifier over the O-Net database.</v>

29
00:03:28.853 --> 00:03:32.135
<v Daniel>That's the giant government catalog of what skills every job requires, right?</v>

30
00:03:32.155 --> 00:03:38.339
<v Maya>Exactly. But here's the key. Their model was strictly bound by three specific engineering bottlenecks of that era.</v>

31
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.480
<v Daniel>Okay. What were they?</v>

32
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.784
<v Maya>Perception and manipulation, creative intelligence, and social intelligence.</v>

33
00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:52.251
<v Daniel>So meaning that at the time, a machine couldn't walk across a messy construction site without falling over. That's perception and manipulation.</v>

34
00:03:52.491 --> 00:03:52.731
<v Maya>Right.</v>

35
00:03:53.151 --> 00:04:01.353
<v Daniel>It couldn't write a compelling novel. So that's creative intelligence. And it couldn't read a client's frustration during negotiation, which is social intelligence.</v>

36
00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:08.074
<v Maya>You nailed it. And if your job heavily required those human traits, Fran Osborne's model flagged your job as safe.</v>

37
00:04:08.854 --> 00:04:14.075
<v Daniel>OK, but this is where the new 2026 Brody paper delivers its first massive course correction, isn't it?</v>

38
00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:28.652
<v Maya>It really is. Section 2 of the Chicago Fed paper argues that those three bottlenecks, they haven't just narrowed. They are rapidly closing. Like the technology has completely leaped over the hurdles that defined the 2017 safe zones.</v>

39
00:04:29.192 --> 00:04:38.474
<v Daniel>I read that section and honestly, the examples they cite are just staggering. Like for perception and manipulation, they point to robotics like Boston Dynamics Atlas.</v>

40
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:40.253
<v Maya>Oh, yeah. Atlas is wild.</v>

41
00:04:40.394 --> 00:04:50.877
<v Daniel>Right. We are no longer talking about a dumb robot arm repeating one motion on an assembly line. Atlas uses multimodal foundation models. It takes in visual and language data simultaneously.</v>

42
00:04:51.497 --> 00:04:58.580
<v Maya>Yeah. So if it drops a heavy box, it dynamically recalculates its balance and just recovers in real time. It's not a programmed script.</v>

43
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:12.956
<v Daniel>Exactly. And then for creative intelligence. Generative AI isn't just making adequate text anymore. It's producing studio-grade music. It's generating jokes that blind human panels are actually rating as funnier than human written comedy.</v>

44
00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:20.951
<v Maya>Which is a little insulting, but true. And then the social intelligence bottleneck. That one is arguably the most disrupted of all.</v>

45
00:05:21.531 --> 00:05:21.831
<v Daniel>Really?</v>

46
00:05:22.151 --> 00:05:36.918
<v Maya>How so? Well, we have large language models consistently outperforming humans on standardized emotion recognition tests now. In medical and financial settings, AI systems are generating these deeply empathetic responses to distressed users.</v>

47
00:05:37.293 --> 00:05:41.176
<v Daniel>Oh, right, where the patients actually prefer the AI's bedside manner over real doctors?</v>

48
00:05:41.396 --> 00:05:41.876
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

49
00:05:42.317 --> 00:05:56.947
<v Daniel>But I have to challenge that just a bit. Does a large language model actually possess social intelligence? Or is it just a hyper-advanced autocomplete that mathematically mapped the most statistically probable empathetic words to</v>

50
00:05:57.207 --> 00:05:57.587
<v Daniel>output?</v>

51
00:05:58.088 --> 00:06:12.981
<v Maya>Well, philosophically, it's... Sure, it's just statistical pattern matching. It doesn't actually feel empathy. Right. But economically, the labor market does not care about the philosophy. If a patient feels heard</v>

52
00:06:13.762 --> 00:06:20.667
<v Maya>and the financial advice is sound, the task of providing empathetic customer service has been successfully executed by the machine.</v>

53
00:06:21.127 --> 00:06:25.651
<v Daniel>Wow. Okay. So the bottleneck is closed regardless of how the machine got there.</v>

54
00:06:26.091 --> 00:06:28.573
<v Maya>Exactly. The output is what matters.</v>

55
00:06:29.175 --> 00:06:43.947
<v Daniel>which fundamentally rewrites the rules of who is at risk. Because if those bottlenecks are gone, who is actually in the crosshairs today? I mean, a decade ago, the prevailing wisdom was that robots were coming for the factory workers, right? The truck drivers, the blue-collar physical</v>

56
00:06:43.987 --> 00:06:44.227
<v Daniel>labor.</v>

57
00:06:44.571 --> 00:06:59.035
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely. We spent a whole decade bracing for a blue-collar impact, but the data shifted radically. And we can see exactly how it shifted in that 2024 paper by Alondu and her team, the one titled GPTs or GPTs.</v>

58
00:06:59.195 --> 00:07:00.315
<v Daniel>Right, the OpenAI study.</v>

59
00:07:00.815 --> 00:07:08.998
<v Maya>Yeah, they created a measure for exposure to large language models, and their map completely flipped the Frey-Osborne narrative upside down.</v>

60
00:07:09.478 --> 00:07:09.818
<v Daniel>How so?</v>

61
00:07:10.545 --> 00:07:19.653
<v Maya>The new data showed that occupations with higher wages, higher barriers to entry and heavy cognitive demands actually had the absolute highest exposure to these models.</v>

62
00:07:20.113 --> 00:07:31.803
<v Daniel>It's like we spent 10 years building this massive impenetrable fortress to protect the workers on the factory floor and we just forgot to build a roof. The AI parachuted directly into the executive suites in the corner offices.</v>

63
00:07:32.158 --> 00:07:42.191
<v Maya>That is the perfect way to put it. It really did. And a 2025 paper by Tomlinson and colleagues proves just how far into the corner office it actually went.</v>

64
00:07:42.446 --> 00:07:44.127
<v Daniel>That's the working with AI paper, right?</v>

65
00:07:44.227 --> 00:07:51.691
<v Maya>Yes. And Tomlinson's team took a totally different approach to measuring risk. They didn't ask experts to guess what AI might do in the future. Oh,</v>

66
00:07:51.731 --> 00:07:52.291
<v Daniel>thank goodness.</v>

67
00:07:52.531 --> 00:08:07.058
<v Maya>Right. Instead, they analyzed 200,000 real-world conversations that people were actively having with Microsoft's Bing co-pilot. They mapped those actual prompts to job tasks to create AI applicability scores.</v>

68
00:08:07.578 --> 00:08:08.999
<v Daniel>And what were the highest scoring fields?</v>

69
00:08:10.046 --> 00:08:13.611
<v Maya>Writing, editing, programming, sales, and customer service.</v>

70
00:08:14.091 --> 00:08:24.604
<v Daniel>The profound irony there is that writing, programming, and sales are the exact jobs Frey and Osborne classified as deeply safe in 2017 because they supposedly required creative and social intelligence.</v>

71
00:08:25.165 --> 00:08:27.248
<v Maya>The irony is very, very rich.</v>

72
00:08:27.772 --> 00:08:40.980
<v Daniel>But wait, hold on. If Tomlinson is just looking at being copilot conversations, isn't there a massive selection bias there? Like they're only measuring what early adopter tech workers are doing with AI, not necessarily with the broader economies actually automating.</v>

73
00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:53.847
<v Maya>That is a very fair critique. It's absolutely an index of early adoption. However, when the Brody paper takes Tomlinson's real world usage data and combines it with actual Bureau of Labor Statistics data.</v>

74
00:08:53.947 --> 00:08:55.368
<v Daniel>Oh, they smash the data sets together.</v>

75
00:08:55.763 --> 00:09:05.699
<v Maya>Exactly. That's when we start to see the actual macroeconomic outcomes. And this is where the new Chicago Fed paper becomes absolutely essential for anyone trying to understand the current economy.</v>

76
00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:17.977
<v Daniel>Okay, let's get into the hard numbers then. The Brody data covering 2019 to 2024. Because if you are putting together that presentation on the future of work we talked about, these are the quotable findings you want on your slides.</v>

77
00:09:18.377 --> 00:09:28.923
<v Maya>For sure. So the researchers took 70 occupations from Tomlinson's co-pilot applicability lists and they mapped them back to the original Frey-Osborne risk scores.</v>

78
00:09:29.566 --> 00:09:38.394
<v Daniel>Just to remind everyone, high risk was a 70% or higher probability of automation. Moderate risk was 30 to 70, and low risk was under 30.</v>

79
00:09:38.714 --> 00:09:50.264
<v Maya>Right. And then they looked at actual employment and wage changes for those groups over five years. And the first major finding just completely shatters the idea that AI exposure equals unemployment.</v>

80
00:09:50.485 --> 00:09:51.926
<v Daniel>Really? What did they find?</v>

81
00:09:52.204 --> 00:10:02.629
<v Maya>According to the data, the occupations with the highest AI applicability, those cognitive white-collar jobs we just talked about, they actually gained about 4% in employment over those five years.</v>

82
00:10:02.849 --> 00:10:03.689
<v Daniel>Wait, gained?</v>

83
00:10:03.829 --> 00:10:08.752
<v Maya>Yes. That translates to an addition of 335,800 jobs. And concurrently, they saw their wages rise about 22%.</v>

84
00:10:12.133 --> 00:10:22.097
<v Daniel>Wait, wait, wait. How does that work? I mean, if a technology can do a huge chunk of a programmer's or writer's job, shouldn't we need fewer of them? Why on earth did employment grow 4%?</v>

85
00:10:22.497 --> 00:10:27.699
<v Maya>Well, what we're likely seeing here is a classic economic mechanism known as the Jevons paradox.</v>

86
00:10:28.239 --> 00:10:29.700
<v Daniel>Okay, give me the economics 101 on that.</v>

87
00:10:30.429 --> 00:10:42.359
<v Maya>So when a technology makes a resource more efficient, we don't actually use less of it. We use much, much more of it. If AI makes a programmer, say, 30% faster, the cost of producing software drops.</v>

88
00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:48.785
<v Daniel>Oh, I see. Because software is now cheaper to produce, companies realize they can afford to build way more of it.</v>

89
00:10:49.005 --> 00:10:56.892
<v Maya>Exactly. So they actually hire more programmers to meet that newly induced demand. The AI acts as a complement to these workers, not a substitute.</v>

90
00:10:57.298 --> 00:11:07.483
<v Daniel>That makes a lot of sense for the cognitive jobs. But what about the routine jobs that were supposed to be automated away entirely? Like what happened to the Frey-Osborne high risk bucket?</v>

91
00:11:08.023 --> 00:11:13.886
<v Maya>Now, this is where their predictions were actually highly accurate in direction, perhaps not in magnitude.</v>

92
00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:15.506
<v Daniel>OK. Give me the numbers.</v>

93
00:11:16.167 --> 00:11:24.871
<v Maya>The group that Frey and Osborne labeled as high risk did see a net loss. They shrank by about 3% in employment. The moderate risk group lost about 6%.</v>

94
00:11:25.131 --> 00:11:25.811
<v Daniel>And the low risk?</v>

95
00:11:26.566 --> 00:11:28.867
<v Maya>the low-risk group grew by about 8%.</v>

96
00:11:29.747 --> 00:11:42.313
<v Daniel>Okay. So the direction is right. But I was looking at the paper, and when you look inside that high-risk bucket, the extremes are absolutely wild. Like telemarketers fell by 51%.</v>

97
00:11:42.573 --> 00:11:43.173
<v Maya>Huge drop.</v>

98
00:11:43.693 --> 00:11:58.161
<v Daniel>And switchboard operators fell by 47%. I mean, that is pure, undeniable automation wiping out routine transactional work. But then... In that exact same high risk category, employment for models like fashion and commercial models</v>

99
00:11:58.701 --> 00:12:00.742
<v Daniel>rose 131%.</v>

100
00:12:01.343 --> 00:12:02.323
<v Maya>Which is wild, right?</v>

101
00:12:02.424 --> 00:12:02.644
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

102
00:12:03.164 --> 00:12:07.507
<v Maya>But it perfectly illustrates that an automation exposure score is not a destiny.</v>

103
00:12:07.991 --> 00:12:08.311
<v Daniel>How so?</v>

104
00:12:08.692 --> 00:12:23.522
<v Maya>Well, a model might be highly exposed to generative AI, creating digital clothing or virtual photo shoots. But if the volume of e-commerce explodes, partly because of AI, the underlying demand for the human baseline of that work might just skyrocket alongside it.</v>

105
00:12:23.802 --> 00:12:38.436
<v Daniel>Wow. OK. But listen, there is one finding in the Brody paper that I simply don't buy on its face. They state that wages rose in every single group. They claim the high-risk group, the group that actively lost jobs, saw a 26% increase in</v>

106
00:12:38.496 --> 00:12:38.896
<v Daniel>wages.</v>

107
00:12:39.576 --> 00:12:40.657
<v Maya>Yeah, that sounds impossible.</v>

108
00:12:40.837 --> 00:12:45.921
<v Daniel>Right. How can wages possibly go up by 26% in a profession that is actively shrinking?</v>

109
00:12:46.762 --> 00:12:54.067
<v Maya>You are right to be skeptical because we have to parse what that number actually represents. First, we're looking at a composition effect.</v>

110
00:12:54.525 --> 00:13:07.196
<v Daniel>Oh, let me guess. It's like a restaurant firing all of its entry level busboys and dishwashers, but keeping the highly paid head chef. So the average salary of a worker at that restaurant just shoots through the roof, even though absolutely nobody got a raise.</v>

111
00:13:07.496 --> 00:13:19.207
<v Maya>That is the exact mechanism. If telemarketing call centers automate all the basic outbound dialing, the only humans left on the payroll are the highly specialized, highly paid tier three escalation managers.</v>

112
00:13:19.878 --> 00:13:31.122
<v Daniel>So the average wage of the remaining occupation goes up mathematically purely because the bottom fell out. Bingo. And isn't there an inflation factor here, too? I mean, the window is 2019 to 2024.</v>

113
00:13:31.462 --> 00:13:40.686
<v Maya>Crucially, yes. A 26 percent wage bump in this study is nominal wage growth. It does not account for the historically high inflation we saw during the pandemic recovery.</v>

114
00:13:41.206 --> 00:13:45.648
<v Daniel>Right. So the purchasing power of that 26 percent is much, much smaller in real terms.</v>

115
00:13:46.027 --> 00:13:53.375
<v Maya>Definitely. Which really brings up the broader limitations of this study. And to be fair, the authors are admirably honest about this.</v>

116
00:13:53.516 --> 00:13:54.256
<v Daniel>Yeah, they don't hide it.</v>

117
00:13:54.657 --> 00:14:04.548
<v Maya>No, they're looking at descriptive percentage changes across just 70 occupations. There is no causal design here. They explicitly call for a difference in differences work in the future.</v>

118
00:14:05.417 --> 00:14:20.097
<v Daniel>And for those of us who haven't taken an econometrics class recently, that basically just means they want future studies to compare a group heavily affected by AI against a control group that wasn't over the exact same time period, right? To prove that AI actually caused</v>

119
00:14:20.397 --> 00:14:20.958
<v Daniel>these changes.</v>

120
00:14:21.316 --> 00:14:30.082
<v Maya>Exactly. Because the 2019 to 2024 window is incredibly noisy. I mean, it includes the massive anomalous shock of a global pandemic.</v>

121
00:14:30.662 --> 00:14:31.282
<v Daniel>Minor detail.</v>

122
00:14:31.683 --> 00:14:41.709
<v Maya>Right. And we have to remember, this data mostly predates the widespread, mature, enterprise rollout of generative AI. We are just looking at the tremors before the main earthquake.</v>

123
00:14:42.485 --> 00:14:57.469
<v Daniel>So if the U.S. data is this heavily muddled by the pandemic and, frankly, by America's at-will employment culture, how do we know if this restructuring is a fundamental rule of AI or just an American fluke? Do we have data from a country with a totally different</v>

124
00:14:57.489 --> 00:14:58.049
<v Daniel>labor structure?</v>

125
00:14:58.509 --> 00:15:09.252
<v Maya>We do. And it is arguably the most robust data set in our entire stack. Hernes and Kostel published a paper in 2026 analyzing the universe of private sector employment in Norway.</v>

126
00:15:09.712 --> 00:15:10.452
<v Daniel>The entire country.</v>

127
00:15:10.666 --> 00:15:17.911
<v Maya>Yes, they aren't looking at a sample. They track the entire country's private sector monthly through a public dashboard all the way up to February 2026. Okay,</v>

128
00:15:17.931 --> 00:15:23.235
<v Daniel>so what actually happened in Norway after ChatGPT launched in late 2022?</v>

129
00:15:23.755 --> 00:15:37.204
<v Maya>Almost nothing dramatic in the aggregate. From October 2022 to early 2026, employment in the most AI-exposed occupations in Norway grew 0.1%. And employment in the least exposed occupations grew 0.3%.</v>

130
00:15:38.746 --> 00:15:40.507
<v Daniel>So it's essentially a flat line across the board.</v>

131
00:15:40.747 --> 00:15:41.148
<v Maya>Pretty much.</v>

132
00:15:41.988 --> 00:15:52.415
<v Daniel>But wait, usually when an industry faces technological disruption, the pain doesn't hit the senior managers first. It hits the entry-level hiring, like the 22-year-olds or the canaries in the coal mine.</v>

133
00:15:52.676 --> 00:15:52.896
<v Maya>Right.</v>

134
00:15:53.496 --> 00:16:01.902
<v Daniel>If AI could do the junior-level coding or basic copywriting, you expect companies to just stop hiring recent graduates. Did the Norwegian data show a youth collapse?</v>

135
00:16:02.735 --> 00:16:14.442
<v Maya>It did not. For young workers in Norway, the relative decline in that most exposed top quintile is an imprecise zero. There is absolutely no massive drop off in entry level hiring.</v>

136
00:16:14.842 --> 00:16:24.408
<v Daniel>That directly contradicts what we saw in the U.S., doesn't it? Because I think Hernes and Kostel actually cite a U.S. finding in their own paper showing the exact opposite.</v>

137
00:16:24.788 --> 00:16:35.691
<v Maya>Good catch. Yes. They cite an analysis of U.S. payroll data from ADP, which found that young workers in the most exposed U.S. occupations are down about 15 log points.</v>

138
00:16:36.132 --> 00:16:37.632
<v Daniel>And in normal human math?</v>

139
00:16:38.172 --> 00:16:52.797
<v Maya>Right. Sorry. A drop of 15 log points translates to roughly a 14 percent decline in employment for those young U.S. workers. That is a highly significant hit to entry level jobs. And by the way, Sweden also saw a decline, though it was smaller than the U.S.</v>

140
00:16:53.567 --> 00:17:08.361
<v Daniel>Okay, why is there a 14% drop in America, a smaller drop in Sweden, and zero drop in Norway? I mean, the technology is identical. A language model doesn't care if it's operating in New York or Oslo. So the difference has to be the human institutions,</v>

141
00:17:08.822 --> 00:17:08.962
<v Daniel>right?</v>

142
00:17:09.526 --> 00:17:20.255
<v Maya>Absolutely. A technology's impact is always filtered through a country's specific labor laws. Norway has a highly organized two-tier bargaining system and exceptionally strong worker protections.</v>

143
00:17:20.515 --> 00:17:23.437
<v Daniel>Meaning there is immense friction involved in firing someone.</v>

144
00:17:23.658 --> 00:17:24.078
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

145
00:17:24.398 --> 00:17:36.228
<v Daniel>You can't just lay off a whole department because you bought a new enterprise AI license. So instead of firing people, Norwegian companies are forced to absorb the shock internally. They probably retrain that entry-level worker to do something else within the firm.</v>

146
00:17:36.817 --> 00:17:49.462
<v Maya>Precisely. Their system buffers the shock through internal reallocation. In the US, where employment is largely at will, a firm can instantly halt entry-level hiring or cut headcount the moment a new software promises efficiency.</v>

147
00:17:50.098 --> 00:17:56.903
<v Daniel>Yeah, the U.S. labor market is just highly fluid, which means the impacts of automation show up in the unemployment data much, much faster.</v>

148
00:17:57.063 --> 00:17:57.683
<v Maya>Exactly right.</v>

149
00:17:58.384 --> 00:18:13.374
<v Daniel>So if we step back and look at this entire landscape, the shifting bottlenecks, the co-pilot real-world usage, the U.S. wage complexities, and the Norwegian institutional buffer, it's time to grade those early, terrifying forecasts. What did the</v>

150
00:18:13.414 --> 00:18:15.956
<v Daniel>Frey and Osborne era predictions actually get right?</v>

151
00:18:16.676 --> 00:18:31.401
<v Maya>Well, they were remarkably accurate about the trajectory of routine, transactional work. The data confirms that roles like telemarketing, switchboard operators, and highly repetitive administrative tasks are indeed shrinking. The vulnerability of those roles was very</v>

152
00:18:31.441 --> 00:18:31.681
<v Maya>real.</v>

153
00:18:32.202 --> 00:18:33.662
<v Daniel>And what did they fundamentally get wrong?</v>

154
00:18:34.242 --> 00:18:44.146
<v Maya>They misjudged the scale, the speed, and the targets. Like, we didn't see a 47% wipeout of the American workforce. And more importantly, they thought cognitive and creative jobs were the safe havens.</v>

155
00:18:44.732 --> 00:18:46.793
<v Daniel>Right, the whole AI can't write a poem thing.</v>

156
00:18:46.873 --> 00:19:00.182
<v Maya>Exactly. The advent of generative AI completely flipped that script, placing high-wage knowledge work directly into the path of automation, while manual, physical jobs remained protected by the sheer, messy complexity of the physical world.</v>

157
00:19:00.342 --> 00:19:15.337
<v Daniel>Which leaves the great unknown, the long-term causal impact. Because the data we have from Brody is a snapshot of... 2019 to 2024. We don't yet know what the labor market looks like in 2030 when enterprise generative AI is</v>

158
00:19:15.437 --> 00:19:17.498
<v Daniel>fully baked into every corporate workflow.</v>

159
00:19:17.518 --> 00:19:18.018
<v Maya>We really don't.</v>

160
00:19:18.918 --> 00:19:31.482
<v Daniel>But we do know enough right now to act. And this is where the findings of this deep dive become incredibly actionable, especially for higher education. Let's talk about the so what for universities.</v>

161
00:19:32.006 --> 00:19:46.675
<v Maya>I'm glad you brought that up because the absolute core conclusion of the Brody paper is a paradigm shift in how we think about automation. They conclude that AI exposure scores should not be read as forecasts of job loss. They are indicators of occupational</v>

162
00:19:46.735 --> 00:19:47.755
<v Maya>restructuring.</v>

163
00:19:47.795 --> 00:19:48.376
<v Daniel>Restructuring.</v>

164
00:19:48.556 --> 00:19:48.776
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

165
00:19:49.036 --> 00:19:51.938
<v Daniel>That one word changes the entire mission of a university, doesn't it?</v>

166
00:19:52.038 --> 00:19:52.558
<v Maya>Completely.</v>

167
00:19:52.819 --> 00:20:06.468
<v Daniel>If a high exposure score just means that day-to-day tasks of a job are going to change radically, then an educator's job is not to panic. Like, you do not tell a student to abandon computer science or graphic design just because AI can write code or generate images.</v>

168
00:20:06.808 --> 00:20:16.675
<v Maya>Exactly. You don't abandon the field. You adapt the curriculum to the restructuring. A university's playbook must focus on preparing students for the changed version of that job.</v>

169
00:20:17.251 --> 00:20:18.993
<v Daniel>So what does that look like in practice?</v>

170
00:20:19.593 --> 00:20:34.426
<v Maya>Well, if AI writes the first draft of the code, you don't teach a student to be a basic coder. You teach them to be an elite AI code reviewer and a systems architect. You teach the writer to be a sophisticated editor of AI generated drafts. The human</v>

171
00:20:34.446 --> 00:20:36.848
<v Maya>baseline just moves up a level of abstraction.</v>

172
00:20:37.633 --> 00:20:50.204
<v Daniel>Which means universities can't design their programs in an academic vacuum anymore. They have to partner directly and continuously with employers to understand how these highly exposed roles are actually being redesigned on the ground.</v>

173
00:20:50.665 --> 00:20:54.888
<v Maya>Yeah, you simply cannot teach the curriculum of 2019 and 2026.</v>

174
00:20:55.209 --> 00:21:00.093
<v Daniel>Right. And we have to be intensely focused on who is most vulnerable during this transition.</v>

175
00:21:00.633 --> 00:21:14.024
<v Maya>And the data tells us exactly who is most vulnerable. It's early career workers, those young people trying to enter restructured fields where the traditional entry-level stepping stones like basic copywriting or entry-level coding are being automated away.</v>

176
00:21:14.443 --> 00:21:26.906
<v Daniel>And it's adult learners, too, right? People who are currently working in those clerical and administrative roles that we know for a fact are shrinking by 40 or 50 percent, they need rapid, accessible pathways to reskill.</v>

177
00:21:27.146 --> 00:21:31.768
<v Maya>Exactly. And perhaps most importantly, it's first generation students.</v>

178
00:21:31.908 --> 00:21:32.748
<v Daniel>Oh, that makes a lot of sense.</v>

179
00:21:32.888 --> 00:21:44.918
<v Maya>Yeah. Navigating a stable job market is hard enough, but when the fundamental tasks of a profession change overnight, success requires immense adaptability and often a network of professional mentors.</v>

180
00:21:45.059 --> 00:21:54.447
<v Daniel>Right. So students without deep built-in social capital need institutions to actively help them pivot when the rules of their chosen industry rewrite themselves mid-degree.</v>

181
00:21:54.507 --> 00:21:56.789
<v Maya>Precisely. They need that structural support.</v>

182
00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:12.826
<v Daniel>You know... This shouldn't be a terrifying prospect. I actually look at the Brody data as a highly empowering insight. It's not about hiding from artificial intelligence. It's about mastering the restructuring. If the furniture in the house is being moved around,</v>

183
00:22:13.487 --> 00:22:16.089
<v Daniel>you want your students to be the ones designing the new floor plan.</v>

184
00:22:16.409 --> 00:22:27.578
<v Maya>Beautifully said. The technology is fundamentally a tool for augmentation in these cognitive fields. And history shows us that those who learn to wield the new tools tend to thrive in the restructured landscape.</v>

185
00:22:28.518 --> 00:22:43.371
<v Daniel>So let's recap the reality check today. We started a decade ago with a forecast that nearly half of all jobs were doomed. We watched the engineering bottlenecks of perception, creativity, and social intelligence rapidly close. We saw the</v>

186
00:22:43.431 --> 00:22:56.222
<v Daniel>risk map completely flip from the blue-collar factory floor to the white-collar cognitive worker. But when we checked the hard data from the Chicago Fed and the Norwegian government, we didn't find an employment apocalypse.</v>

187
00:22:56.853 --> 00:23:11.826
<v Maya>No, we found unexpected job growth in highly AI-applicable fields, thanks to the Jevons paradox. We untangled the illusion of 26% wage bumps. And we uncovered a massive story of complex occupational restructuring rather</v>

188
00:23:11.846 --> 00:23:19.532
<v Maya>than pure destruction. The storm definitely hit, but the house is still standing. It just functions entirely differently on the inside.</v>

189
00:23:20.293 --> 00:23:34.605
<v Daniel>Which brings me back to a final thought for you to chew on. If AI doesn't erase your field, but instead radically changes the daily tasks required to do it, well, will the future of education be less about earning a static one-time degree</v>

190
00:23:35.327 --> 00:23:38.373
<v Daniel>and more about proving your continuous rate of adaptability?</v>

191
00:23:39.294 --> 00:23:42.561
<v Maya>That is the defining challenge for the next decade of higher education.</v>

192
00:23:42.821 --> 00:23:50.136
<v Daniel>Something to think about the next time you look at a curriculum or a career path. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive. We'll catch you next time.</v>
